<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2210_05475_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2210.05475_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2210.05475_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2210_05475_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "noise [ 47 , 57 ]. A crucial drawback of DDMs is that the generative ODE or SDE is typically difcult to solve, due to the complex score function. Therefore, efcient and tailored samplers are required for fast synthesis. In this work, building on the generative ODE [ 47 , 57 , 58 ], we rigorously derive a novel second-order ODE solver using truncated Taylor methods [ 59 ]. These higher-order methods require higher-order gradients of the ODEin our case this includes higher-order gradients of the W" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2210_05475_rag__chunk_015> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2210.05475_rag::chunk_015" ;
    kg:docId "2210.05475_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2210_05475_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "6 Conclusions We introduced GENIE, a higher-order ODE solver for DDMs. GENIE improves upon the commonly used DDIM solver by capturing the local curvature of its ODEs gradient eld, which allows for larger step sizes when solving the ODE. We further propose to distill the required higher-order derivatives into a small prediction headwhich we can efciently call during inferenceon top of the rst-order score network. A limitation of GENIE is that it is still slightly slower than approaches that aband" .

