    :hasEvidenceText "A diffusion model typically consists of two Markov chains of T steps. One of them is the forward process"^^xsd:string,
        "A diffusion model typically consists of two Markov chains of T steps. One of them is the forward processalso known as the diffusion processwhich incrementally adds Gaussian noises"^^xsd:string,
        "Conventional diffusion models learn the diffusion process flatly for each class"^^xsd:string,
        "DDMs then learn to revert the forward process p ( x t 1 | x t ) with learnable parameters ."^^xsd:string,
        "Denoising diffusion models have emerged as a dominant approach for image generation, however they still suffer from slow convergence in training"^^xsd:string,
        "DiT-XL/2 outperforms all prior diffusion models"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models (Sohl-Dickstein et al. 2015; Song and Ermon 2019) work by inverting a stepwise noise process using latent variables."^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models [ 25 , 62 , 67 ] are powerful deep generative models that emerge recently for high quality image generation"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models [ 82 , 213 ] are a category of probabilistic generative models that learn to reverse a process that gradually degrades the training data structure"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models are a class of probabilistic generative models that learn to reverse a process that gradually degrades"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models frequently employ U-Net models as denoisers"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models generate samples by reversing a xed forward diffusion process."^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models have achieved great success in image synthesis through iterati"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models have significantly advanced the fields of image, audio, and video generation, but they depend on an iterative sampling process that causes slow generation."^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models may be viewed as hierarchical variational autoencoders (VAEs) with two improvements: parameter sharing for the conditional distributions in the generative process"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion models posit a forward diffusion process { z t }"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion probabilistic models (DPMs) have been shown to generate high-quality images"^^xsd:string,
        "Diffusion processes have been recently utilized to construct Diffusion Models , a class of generative models in deep learning"^^xsd:string,
        "Due to the nature of generative models, diffusion models are convenient for diverse denoising problems"^^xsd:string,
        "However, there is a class of models, known as diffusion models"^^xsd:string,
        "In the latter steps of generation, diffusion models take charge of detail generation"^^xsd:string,
        "Many methods based on diffusion models have been developed for text generation."^^xsd:string,
        "Neural diffusion models Finally, the reverse distribution is set to p"^^xsd:string,
        "Our method accelerates diffusion models by using different-sized models to process different steps."^^xsd:string,
        "Training diffusion models is extremely resource intensive"^^xsd:string,
        "We explore a new class of diffusion models based on the transformer architecture."^^xsd:string,
        "We feed this to the diffusion model via cross-attention"^^xsd:string,
        "a cascade of diffusion models for generating high-resolution images. These models are also conditioned on the text embeddings returned by the encoder."^^xsd:string,
        "a diffusion model is trained to denoise the noised latent"^^xsd:string,
        "a diffusion-based decoder that can generate an image conditioned on the image embedding."^^xsd:string,
        "analyze the general properties of diffusion models"^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion in the latent space such that longer videos with more than one thousand frames can be produced."^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models [ 20 , 50 ] have emerged as a leading solution for image generation [ 16 , 35 ], and for text-to-image synthesis"^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models are also notoriously expensive and data-hungry to train."^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models are also notoriously expensive and data-hungry to train. They require large datasets and many iterations to capture the high-dimensional and complex data distributions."^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models can be understood as hierarchical variational autoencoders (VAEs) (Kingma & Welling, 2013; Rezende et al., 2014; Snderby et al., 2016)"^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models can capture effectively complex conditional probability distributions and apply them to solve problems such as in-painting, super resolution or image-to-image translation"^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models have a stable training process and provide more diversity because they are likelihoodbased."^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models have been applied to a wide range of generative NLP tasks"^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models have emerged as a very powerful class of generative models, capable of generating high-"^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models, especially in the resource constrained environments."^^xsd:string,
        "diffusion models. The essence of this method lies in learning an ordinary differential equation that follows a path from the source distribution to the target distribution."^^xsd:string,
        "discarding samples with small loss values in diffusion model training, thus mitigating the memorization."^^xsd:string,
        "fast sampling of diffusion models"^^xsd:string,
        "generative model is tasked at recovering the original input data from the diffused (noisy) data by learning to gradually reverse the diffusion process"^^xsd:string,
        "generative model is tasked at recovering the original input data from the diffused (noisy) data by learning to gradually reverse the diffusion process, step by step."^^xsd:string,
        "increasing model size and decreasing patch size yields considerably improved diffusion models."^^xsd:string,
        "multiple steps (e.g., 25-1000) to generate a sample [11]. This is a potential hindrance in the practical applications of diffusion models, especially in the resource constrained environments."^^xsd:string,
        "noise-based diffusion models, but they rely on score matching during the reverse process."^^xsd:string,
        "pre-trained diffusion model to be distilled"^^xsd:string,
        "single denoising step can be regarded as a conventional deep learning model inference"^^xsd:string,
        "the noise prediction task in diffusion models"^^xsd:string,
        "to train the diffusion model. The model training in practice is similar to reducing the variational upper bound on the negative log probability."^^xsd:string ;
